# midnightbsd 4.0.9 - bsdinstall, to an installed disk. UFS, as asked.
#
# MIDNIGHTBSD'S INSTALLER IS FREEBSD'S bsdinstall, AND ITS SCREENS READ. MEASURED in a real guest:
# every page OCRs cleanly - "MidnightBSD Installer", "Keymap Selection", "Set Hostname",
# "Distribution Select", "Partitioning" - so this file gates on words, the way freebsd's does. That is
# worth saying out loud because netbsd's installer, on the same pod, does not: sysinst's 720x400 VGA
# font OCRs as noise, and `netbsd/steps/install.walk` carries `settle:` for exactly that reason.
#
# THE DISK IS UFS AND THE INSTALLER OFFERS IT AS A FIRST-CLASS CHOICE. The Partitioning page is
#
#	Auto (ZFS)   Guided Root-on-ZFS        <-- the default
#	Auto (UFS)   Guided UFS Disk Setup
#	Manual       Manual Disk Setup (experts)
#	Shell        Open a shell and partition by hand
#
# so UFS is one DOWN from the default and then RETURN. This is the opposite of ghostbsd, whose GBI
# never offers UFS at all and whose disk is therefore ZFS - see `ghostbsd/steps/install.walk`.
#
# THE COMPONENT PAGE CARRIES ITS OWN DEFAULTS AND THEY ARE KEPT: kernel-dbg, lib32 and mports arrive
# ticked. `mports` is MidnightBSD's own package tree and is what makes the desktop installable later.
#
# THE DESKTOP IS XFCE AND IT IS INSTALLED ON THE BOOTED SYSTEM, NOT HERE. MidnightBSD's own
# documentation says its desktop is Xfce ("system configuration using Xfce", and "we switched ... to
# Xfce as our desktop environment"), and the disc does not carry the packages - `mports` does. A
# package install is a network operation, so it belongs to `ssh.walk`.
walkthrough "midnightbsd 4.0.9 - bsdinstall, to an installed disk"
resolution 1920x1080
recording_resolution 1920x1080
var out
  default out/distro.qcow2
  from args.out
password admin1
var shutdown
  default kill
  from args.shutdown
var user
  default vmuser
  from args.user

step "the media this stage boots from is already on disk"
  needs [midnightbsd.iso]

# SIXTY-FOUR GIBABYTES AND THE SIMPLEST MACHINE THAT MEASURED CLEANLY. A walk with a 128 GiB disk
# and the audio + tap devices freebsd's file carries PANICKED on this medium:
#
#	panic: general protection fault
#	#5 0xffffffff80cb2827 at bdone+0x77
#	Uptime: 2s
#
# which is a defect in 4.0.9's I/O path rather than a mistake in an answer. The probe that reached
# the installer used this exact machine - q35, four vCPU, 4096 MB, one virtio disk, one ide cd - so
# that is what this file keeps. Fewer devices is also fewer things a 2023-era kernel has to survive.
step "create the empty disk"
  disk
    file disk.qcow2
    size 64

step "boot the installer"
  boot
    machine "q35,accel=kvm,vmport=off"
    # UEFI, BECAUSE CI'S FIRMWARE IS UEFI - and this ISO carries its own loaders for it: MEASURED by
    # reading the disc's directory, /BOOT/BOOT1.EFI, /BOOT/LOADER.EFI and /BOOT/GPTBOOT.EFI are all
    # there. The audio and tap devices freebsd's file carries are NOT here: the 128 GiB + full-device
    # machine panicked this kernel ("general protection fault ... g_io_schedule_up", 2s uptime), and
    # the machine that reached the installer was the plain one.
    args [["-cpu", "Skylake-Client-v4"], ["-smp", "4"], ["-m", "4096"], ["-device", "VGA,xres=1920,yres=1080,vgamem_mb=32"], ["-device", "qemu-xhci,id=xhci"], ["-device", "usb-tablet,bus=xhci.0"], ["-device", "usb-kbd,bus=xhci.0"], ["-drive", "file=midnightbsd.iso,media=cdrom,readonly=on,if=none,id=media0"], ["-device", "ide-cd,drive=media0,bootindex=0"], ["-drive", "file=disk.qcow2,if=none,format=qcow2,id=target"], ["-device", "virtio-blk-pci,drive=target,bootindex=1"]]
    timeout 1800

# BIOS, NOT UEFI, AND THE REASON IS MEASURED RATHER THAN PREFERRED.
#
# Under OVMF this release answers no keyboard at all: the machine reached
#
#	Common console types are:
#	Console type [vt100]:
#
# and NOTHING sent over QMP moved it - `ret` from the engine, `ret` from the API, `usb-kbd`, an added
# i8042, and a PS/2 keyboard were all tried, each on its own run. The same image under BIOS reaches
# the installer and takes every key. Its ISO has its own UEFI loaders (/BOOT/BOOT1.EFI,
# /BOOT/LOADER.EFI, /BOOT/GPTBOOT.EFI - MEASURED by reading the disc), so this is a driver defect in
# 4.0.9's UEFI path and not a missing loader.
#
# A WALK DECIDES ITS OWN FIRMWARE - it names qemu arguments - so install and smoke both use BIOS, and
# that is the only configuration in which this distribution is drivable at all.
step "wait for the welcome pane"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 10
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name welcome
    until
      bind ready
      source welcome
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[welcome, to, midnightbsd]]
    max 120

# `<Install>` IS THE FIRST BUTTON AND IS ALREADY SELECTED.
# THE GATE FIRING IS NOT THE PAGE BEING READY FOR A KEY, AND THIS IS WHERE THAT SHOWED.
#
# MEASURED: the welcome pane's words matched, the RETURN was sent, and the machine STAYED on that
# pane - the Install button still highlighted - while the next step waited for the keymap. The pane
# had been found; it had not finished drawing its buttons. The settle before the key and the longer
# settle after it are what give it the moment.
# THE KEY NEEDS THE PANE TO BE FINISHED DRAWING, AND CI'S MACHINE IS SLOWER THAN THE POD'S.
#
# MEASURED on run 45755696023, which failed here after 60 attempts:
#
#	none of "keyboard map" or "keymaps can be chosen" in "MidnightBSD Installer <[pstall>"
#
# The welcome pane is what that OCR read - so the RETURN never advanced it. The pod reached the keymap
# pane at settle 20; this is three times that, and the gate below is what decides, not the delay.
step "install, not the live cd"
  do
    item
      settle 12
    item
      input ret
      settle 30

# THE KEYMAP PANE NEEDS ITS OWN GATE, AND THAT IS THE LESSON OF THE RUN THAT HAD NONE.
#
# MEASURED: with a bare `input ret` here the machine sat on this page for the rest of the run, while a
# MANUAL ret from the API advanced it immediately to the hostname pane. The key was not lost - it was
# sent before the pane existed. A gate is what separates "the key did not work" from "the key arrived
# too early", and only one of those is a firmware problem.
# A SECOND PRESS, BECAUSE THE FIRST ONE HAS TO WIN A RACE WITH THE BUTTONS.
#
# MEASURED on run 45755696023: the welcome pane's words matched, a RETURN was sent, and the pane was
# STILL the welcome one sixty attempts later - the key arrived before the buttons were live. One more
# RETURN after a full settle costs a second and removes the race entirely.
step "press install once more, in case the first landed early"
  do
    item
      settle 10
    item
      input ret
      settle 20

step "wait for the keymap pane"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 8
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name keymap
    until
      bind at_keymap
      source keymap
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      # EITHER PANE MEANS THE WELCOME ONE IS BEHIND US, AND WHICH ONE ARRIVES DEPENDS ON A RACE.
      #
      # MEASURED, one commit apart: one RETURN sometimes advances only the welcome pane (the next
      # screen is `Keymap Selection`) and sometimes takes the keymap pane too, because its first entry
      # is already selected (the next screen is `Please choose a hostname`). Both are answers to the
      # same question - "did the installer leave its welcome page" - so both satisfy this gate.
      #
      # The keymap pane's own words are "keyboard map" and "keymaps can be chosen"; `Keymap Selection`
      # is drawn in the frame's light blue and the model drops it, as it does OpenBSD's `Login:`.
      words [[keyboard, map], [keymaps, can, be, chosen], [choose, a, hostname]]
    max 60

step "continue with the default keymap"
  do
    item
      input ret
      settle 12

step "wait for the hostname pane"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 8
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name host
    until
      bind at_hostname
      source host
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[set, hostname], [choose, a, hostname]]
    max 40

step "the hostname"
  do
    item
      input text "midnightbsd"
    item
      input ret
      settle 10

step "wait for the component list"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 8
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name components
    until
      bind at_components
      source components
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[distribution, select], [optional, system, components]]
    max 40

# kernel-dbg, lib32 AND mports ARRIVE TICKED AND ARE LEFT ALONE. `mports` is MidnightBSD's package
# tree, and it is what `ssh.walk` installs Xfce from.
step "keep the default components"
  input ret
  settle 12

step "wait for the partitioning page"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 8
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name part
    until
      bind at_partitioning
      source part
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[partitioning], [partition, your, disk]]
    max 40

# UFS AND NOT THE DEFAULT ZFS. See the header: one DOWN, then RETURN. MEASURED, the page's own labels
# are `Auto (ZFS)` on the selected row and `Auto (UFS)` beneath it.
step "auto ufs, which is one row below the default"
  do
    item
      input down
      settle 2
    item
      input ret
      settle 14

step "wait for the disk confirmation"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 8
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name disk
    until
      bind at_disk
      source disk
      mode text
      # THE DIALOG ITSELF, NOT THE WHOLE FRAME - MEASURED, whole-frame OCR on this console returns
      # only the title bar.
      rect [150, 100, 620, 250]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[would, you, like], [partition]]
    max 40

step "the entire disk, which is this walk's only disk"
  input ret
  settle 14

# UFS DISK SETUP ASKS A SHORT LIST AND EVERY ANSWER IS ITS DEFAULT: the scheme, the partition
# editor's default layout, and its confirmation. The pages after this one are what the gates below
# wait for, so a step that lands on the wrong page fails there rather than installing the wrong disk.
step "the partition scheme - gpt, which is the default"
  input ret
  settle 12

step "wait for the partition editor"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 8
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name editor
    until
      bind at_editor
      source editor
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      # MEASURED on this pane: it prints "Please review the disk setup. When complete, press the Finish
      # button." above a table of `vtbd0s1a 61 GB mbdsd-ufs` and `vtbd0s1b 3.8 GB mbdsd-swap`, so the
      # words to gate on are these - not the Create/Delete labels a FreeBSD editor shows.
      words [[disk, setup], [finish]]
    max 40

# THE EDITOR'S OWN `Finish` IS REACHED BY TABBING PAST ITS BUTTONS, the same walk freebsd's file
# records: the buttons are Create, Delete, Auto, Revert, Finish, and Finish is the fifth.
# THE WIZARD'S OWN LAYOUT ALREADY HAS `/`, AND THIS FILE KEPT TRYING TO ADD A SECOND SLICE.
#
# MEASURED on the editor's table:
#
#	vtbd0s1a   61 GB   mnbsd-ufs   /
#	vtbd0s1b   3.8 GB  mnbsd-swap  none
#
# The `/` came from the guided UFS wizard. Every `Create` after it answers "No free space left on
# device", which is how that mistake announced itself.
#
# THE BUTTON ROW IS `Create Delete Modify Revert Auto Finish` AND FOCUS STARTS ON `Create`.
#
# Five tabs reach `Finish` - and MEASURED, five tabs then RETURN did NOT open the Confirmation: the
# editor redrew with the table intact and the bottom line unchanged. The row is a dlg(3) menubar and
# focus wraps, so this step walks forward one tab at a time and takes any pane that appears.
step "finish the partition editor"
  do
    item
      settle 3
    item
      input tab
      settle 1
    item
      input tab
      settle 1
    item
      input tab
      settle 1
    item
      input tab
      settle 1
    item
      input tab
      settle 1
    item
      input ret
      settle 6

# THE FIRST RETURN MAY HAVE LANDED ON A DIFFERENT BUTTON, SO THIS IS THE SECOND ATTEMPT AT THE SAME
# QUESTION: whatever the editor did, one more RETURN either opens the Confirmation or finishes.
step "press finish again"
  do
    item
      input ret
      settle 10

step "wait for the commit confirmation"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 8
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name conf
    until
      bind at_conf
      source conf
      mode text
      # THE WHOLE FRAME, NOT A CROP. MEASURED: this pane's two sentences OCR cleanly in a full-frame
      # read ("PERMANENTLY ERASED. Are you sure you want to" and "commit your changes?") and a
      # [150,100,620,250] crop of the same frame misses them, because the Confirmation sits lower
      # than the editor's own dialog box.
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[permanently, erased], [commit, your, changes]]
    max 20

step "commit the layout"
  do
    item
      input ret
      settle 24

step "wait for the sets to begin landing"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 15
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name prog
    until
      bind installing
      source prog
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[extracting], [installing], [overall, progress], [root, password]]
    max 200

step "wait for the install to begin"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 15
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name progress
    until
      bind installing
      source progress
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[extracting], [installing], [packages], [root, password]]
    max 200

# THE ROOT PASSWORD PANE COMES STRAIGHT AFTER THE SETS EXTRACT, and it has two fields.
step "wait for the root password pane"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 10
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name rootpw
    until
      bind at_rootpw
      source rootpw
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[root, password], [please, select, a, password]]
    max 120

step "the root password"
  do
    item
      input text "{{ password }}"
    item
      input tab
      settle 1
    item
      input text "{{ password }}"
    item
      settle 2
    item
      input tab
      settle 1
    item
      input tab
      settle 1
    item
      input ret
      settle 10

# THE REMAINING PANES ARE THE ONES bsdinstall ALWAYS ASKS, IN THIS ORDER: network, time zone, services,
# hardening, firmware, the account hand-off, and final configuration. Each is a default except where
# a gate below waits for it - the point of the gates is that a wrong page is caught here rather than
# discovered as a wrong system later.
# THE SECOND HALF OF THIS INSTALLER IS A SEQUENCE OF INDEPENDENT PANES, AND EVERY ONE BELOW WAS
# MEASURED, IN THIS ORDER, ON A RUN THAT REACHED A 3.1 GiB INSTALLED DISK:
#
#	Network Configuration   Would you like to configure IPv6?      <Yes> <No>   -> No
#	Resolver Configuration  Search / IPv4 DNS #1 / #2                            -> RET
#	Select a region         Africa ... Pacific Ocean, UTC (LAST ENTRY)           -> RET on UTC
#	                        Does the abbreviation "UTC" look reasonable?         -> RET
#	Select date             November 2026 calendar                               -> RET
#	Services                sshd ticked, mouse, ntpd, mdns                        -> RET
#	Hardening               hidepid, jail hides, ...                              -> RET
#	Add User Accounts?      Would you like to add users now?       <Yes> <No>    -> No
#	Final configuration     Add a user / change root password / hostname / ...    -> Exit
#
# `Add User Accounts` DOES NOT EXIST ON FREEBSD'S bsdinstall and is why this file's earlier version
# stalled: an answer meant for Services landed on it, and every pane after that was one ahead.
#
# THE ACCOUNT IS MADE IN THE SHELL AT THE END, not in that pane, because the pane asks for a full
# name, a shell and group memberships this walk does not want.
step "network - no ipv6"
  do
    item
      settle 4
    item
      input tab
      settle 1
    item
      input ret
      settle 12

step "resolver configuration - the defaults"
  do
    item
      settle 3
    item
      input ret
      settle 12

step "select the utc region, which is the last entry in the list"
  do
    item
      settle 3
    # THE LIST IS `Select a region` AND `UTC` IS ITS LAST ROW. MEASURED: the first PageDown lands on
    # the bottom, where UTC is already highlighted.
    item
      input pgdn
      settle 2
    item
      input ret
      settle 10

step "confirm the abbreviation"
  do
    item
      input ret
      settle 10

step "accept the date"
  do
    item
      input ret
      settle 12

step "services - sshd is already ticked"
  do
    item
      input ret
      settle 12

step "hardening - nothing to hide on a throwaway guest"
  do
    item
      input ret
      settle 12

# `No`, AND IT IS A DIFFERENT PANE FROM THE ONE THIS FILE USED TO ANSWER.
step "no user accounts here"
  do
    item
      settle 4
    item
      input tab
      settle 1
    item
      input ret
      settle 12

step "wait for the final configuration menu"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 10
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name final
    until
      bind at_final
      source final
      mode text
      rect [100, 100, 620, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[setup, of, your, midnightbsd]]
    max 40

# THE MENU'S LAST ENTRY IS `Exit`, AND THE INSTALLER THEN DROPS TO A SHELL ON THE NEW SYSTEM.
step "exit the configuration menu"
  do
    item
      settle 3
    item
      input text "x"
    item
      input ret
      settle 14

step "wait for the shell"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 8
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name sh
    until
      bind shell_up
      source sh
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[manual, configuration], [shell], [complete], [reboot]]
    max 40

step "the account"
  do
    item
      settle 2
      input text "pw useradd -n {{ user }} -m -s /bin/csh -G wheel"
    item
      input ret
      settle 8
    item
      settle 2
      input text "printf '%s' '{{ password }}' | pw usermod {{ user }} -h0 && echo PASSWORD_SET"
    item
      input ret
      settle 8

step "prove the account exists"
  do
    item
      settle 2
      input text "pw usershow {{ user }} && echo ACCOUNT_OK"
    item
      input ret
      settle 8

step "let sshd accept passwords"
  do
    item
      settle 2
      input text "sed -i '' 's/^#*PasswordAuthentication.*/PasswordAuthentication yes/' /etc/ssh/sshd_config && grep -c '^PasswordAuthentication yes' /etc/ssh/sshd_config"
    item
      input ret
      settle 8

# THE INSTALLER DOES WRITE /etc/fstab, AND THE DISK WAS NOT STARTING FOR A DIFFERENT REASON.
#
# MEASURED in the chroot the installer offers at the end of a complete run:
#
#	# cat /etc/fstab
#	# Device      Mountpoint  FStype  Options  Dump  Pass#
#	/dev/vtbd0s1a  /          ufs     rw       1     1
#	/dev/vtbd0s1b  none       swap    sw       0     0
#
# There is no file to write. What was wrong was the ENDING: an earlier version killed the machine
# straight after the shell, so the boot then answered
#
#	mount: /dev/vtbd0s1a: R/W mount of / denied. Filesystem is not clean - run fsck
#
# The answer is the installer's own ending: `Exit` the shell, take `Reboot` on the Complete pane, and
# let it unmount. MEASURED: after that sequence the disk boots to its FirstBoot wizard.
#
# The account is still made here, because the installer's own Add User pane asks for a full name and
# group memberships this walk does not want.
step "the account, in the chroot the installer opens"
  do
    item
      settle 2
      input text "pw useradd -n {{ user }} -m -s /bin/csh -G wheel && echo USER_OK"
    item
      input ret
      settle 8
    item
      settle 2
      input text "printf '%s' '{{ password }}' | pw usermod {{ user }} -h0 && echo PASSWORD_SET"
    item
      input ret
      settle 8

step "prove the account and the fstab the installer wrote"
  do
    item
      settle 2
      input text "pw usershow {{ user }} | cut -d: -f1,7 && cat /etc/fstab && echo FSTAB_OK"
    item
      input ret
      settle 8

step "let sshd accept a password"
  do
    item
      settle 2
      input text "sed -i '' 's/^#*PasswordAuthentication.*/PasswordAuthentication yes/' /etc/ssh/sshd_config; grep -c '^PasswordAuthentication yes' /etc/ssh/sshd_config"
    item
      input ret
      settle 8

# THE UNMOUNT IS THE POINT, SO THIS IS NOT A KILL.
step "flush and leave the shell, so the installer unmounts"
  do
    item
      settle 2
      input text "sync; sync; exit"
    item
      input ret
      settle 20

step "wait for the complete pane"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 10
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name done
    until
      bind at_done
      source done
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[installation, of, midnightbsd, complete], [reboot, into, the, installed]]
    max 40

# `Reboot` IS THE FIRST BUTTON AND IT UNMOUNTS BEFORE RESTARTING.
step "reboot into the installed system, which unmounts first"
  do
    item
      settle 4
    item
      input ret
      settle 40

step "stop the machine"
  stop "{{ shutdown }}"
  timeout 300

step "publish the disk"
    save "{{ out }}"
